--TEST--
`Core\Cli::arguments` is empty for a program started with no words of its own, rather than answering the process's own argument vector — spec § 15, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other side of the bound, and the one that says where the words come
// from: this program is run by a binary that has a full argument vector of its
// own -- the subcommand and this file's path are on it -- and the member reads
// none of it. `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door` keeps `argv` out of `nvs-stdlib` entirely, so what
// is answered here is what the launcher wrote with `Ctx::set_command_line`,
// which for a case with no `--ARGS--` section is nothing at all.
array<tainted string> $words = Core\Cli::arguments();
echo Core\Arr::count($words), "\n";

// Empty rather than absent: it is an `array<tainted string>` a `foreach` walks
// zero times, never a `null` a program has to test for. A member whose absence
// is a value would be `?T` by `rule:core-api/shape-rules`, and this one has no absence.
foreach ($words as tainted string $word) {
    echo "unreachable:", $word, "\n";
}
echo "walked\n";
--EXPECT--
0
walked
